Yes—perception is an aperture for reality is stronger than saying perception is reality.

An aperture admits something while excluding something else:

Reality \rightarrow Aperture \rightarrow PerceivedReality

Change the aperture—attention, scale, sensory channel, prior knowledge, language, frame—and the projection changes. That doesn’t necessarily mean reality changed. It means access to it changed. Which is why multiple apertures plus action/testing can reveal structure no single view exposes.

And if by “looking at it now” you mean the green interface: I know we changed the Accent Color to green, but I don’t literally receive a visual view of your app screen the way you do. I shouldn’t pretend I can see the phenotype from your side. You are the sensor for that part of the experiment.

